---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/smsbump-alternative';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'alternatives/smsbump-alternative');
const desc = description(site, 'alternatives/smsbump-alternative');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can we still export from SMSBump?",
    "answer": "The current lifecycle notice says the SMS dashboard and background services are inactive. Start with merchant-held archives; do not plan around renewed export access."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can we keep the old phone number?",
    "answer": "Ask the receiving provider to confirm number type, ownership evidence, transfer eligibility and inbound routing. A familiar number or old invoice does not prove a transfer can be completed."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which replacement should we assess first?",
    "answer": "Evaluate Postscript for a Shopify SMS operating workflow, or Klaviyo if the email team will also own SMS. Demonstrate replies, permission mapping and purchaser exits on your account before choosing."
  },
  {
    "question": "What does the retained 16% discount cost?",
    "answer": "On a $622 basket at 34% gross margin, fixed goods cost is $410.52. Discounted revenue is $522.48 and gross profit is $111.96, compared with $211.48 at full price. The $99.52 loss excludes other costs."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-16 container">
<a href="/alternatives" class="link">← All alternatives</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">SMSBump alternatives: rebuild phone and reply continuity</h1>
<p class="mt-6 text-xl">Replace the retired SMS operation from records you actually hold. A new sender needs a verified audience, a working reply destination and fresh cart eligibility, not just an imported phone column.</p>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-10">

<h2>Retirement changes the starting point</h2>
<p>Sources checked November 25, 2026. Yotpo’s <a href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">migration announcement</a> set December 31, 2025 as the full Email and SMS deprecation date. The <a href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">current SMS lifecycle notice</a> confirms the application is decommissioned and dashboard and background services are inactive. An old SMSBump installation is therefore an archive and continuity problem, not a plan to continue sending there.</p>
<p>Inventory merchant-held CSVs, suppression backups, signup screenshots, number invoices, support tickets and campaign copy. Record the date and originating account of each archive. A historical subscriber count cannot establish how many people can receive a message today. Keep an untouched archive and create a separate working import with unresolved rows quarantined. Support may clarify account history; this page does not promise restored access or a new export.</p>
<h2>Give each phone record a usable meaning</h2>
<p>Map country, normalized phone, Shopify customer identifier, permission purpose and channel, source, timestamp and latest opt-out. Retain the original value beside the normalized value so a country-code guess is visible. When two records collapse onto one phone, resolve their suppression history before allowing a send; merging identifiers must not erase a later opt-out. An email subscriber or a checkout phone supplied for delivery is not automatically an SMS marketing subscriber.</p>
<p>Compare archive dates with later merchant-held helpdesk requests and suppression records. An old campaign segment can contain people who withdrew afterward. If evidence cannot settle eligibility, exclude the row from the sending import while preserving the audit trail. Do not use a promotional welcome text to discover whether an uncertain old number still belongs to the same person.</p>
<h2>Choose a replacement around the operator’s job</h2>
<p><a href="https://apps.shopify.com/postscript-sms-marketing">Postscript’s current Shopify listing</a> describes SMS campaigns, automations and two-way messaging, alongside RCS and a phased email rollout. It is a useful candidate for a team rebuilding a dedicated Shopify SMS workflow. Ask for a demonstration of your number arrangement, keyword routing and reply handling; email availability and number transfer still need account-specific confirmation.</p>
<p><a href="https://apps.shopify.com/klaviyo-email-marketing">Klaviyo lists email, SMS and WhatsApp</a>. Evaluate it when the email operator will coordinate the phone channel too. The useful comparison is whether the same purchase and suppression state stops both queues, and whether the team can find and handle a shopper reply. A multi-channel listing does not itself prove that an old SMSBump audience, number or automation transfers intact.</p>
<p>If replacement signup forms are also needed, <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/omnisend">Omnisend lists forms, email/SMS and abandoned-cart automation</a>. Use a mobile signup and its resulting permission fields as the evaluation brief. Compare the chosen scope and actual destination-country charges with your expected sends; no universal price or recovery ranking follows from these listings.</p>
<h2>Rehearse phone, keyword and reply continuity</h2>
<h3>A number printed on packaging</h3>
<p>List every printed number and keyword on packaging, receipts, store signs and landing pages. Ask the receiving provider for a written porting decision and routing sequence. If continuity cannot be confirmed, replace controllable surfaces and assign an owner to printed stock. Do not tell customers to text an old destination merely because the outbound brand name looks familiar. Test inbound delivery from a real test handset after setup, with the receiving inbox and responder identified.</p>
<h3>A keyword that once issued a coupon</h3>
<p>Rebuild the keyword’s purpose, confirmation text and eligibility rule from retained copy. A keyword must not silently change a help request into marketing enrollment. Submit a new test signup, an already-suppressed test record and an unknown keyword. Inspect the resulting state and any coupon issuance. The acceptance evidence is the recorded route and permission decision, not just a delivered welcome message.</p>
<h3>A reply arrives after the cart changed</h3>
<p>Have a test shopper reply with a size question, then place an order before the responder acts. The operator needs current order and cart context so a support reply does not trigger another abandoned-cart offer. Assign a response owner and escalation path for missed replies and opt-outs. Rehearse an opt-out in the receiving system and verify it blocks the next queued marketing message; retain the suppression evidence separately from message history.</p>
<h2>Cart failures worth carrying into the new workflow</h2>
<p>These are proposed acceptance exercises, not observed vendor outcomes. For an out-of-stock variant, remove the selected size after abandonment. Open the recovery link and verify the chosen variant is still purchasable before offering a code; if it is not, provide an accurate product route or stop the offer. A different in-stock size is not a valid restoration of the original basket.</p>
<p>For shipping shock, use a real destination and compare the live shipping quote with the message’s claim. A shipping threshold should use the discounted eligible subtotal, not an old total. For a gift-size question, link to sizing and exchange information and route the reply to support. Neither failure is repaired merely by increasing the coupon.</p>
<p>For a stacked coupon or bundle, restore the cart with its existing automatic discount and inspect the final checkout total. For MOQ, reduce quantity below the merchant’s actual minimum and verify that the message does not promise an impossible purchase. For a mobile or BNPL interruption, test the destination on a phone, including expired checkout state and payment-method availability; avoid claims of saved payment details or guaranteed financing.</p>
<p>For subscription and membership baskets, distinguish the initial order, renewal and member-only eligibility. A cart reminder must not turn a recurring item into a one-time purchase or imply a nonmember qualifies for member pricing. For a repeat buyer, verify a completed order exits the recovery queue even if a delayed old cart event arrives afterward. Store the event identifiers used for deduplication and make one system responsible for each active journey.</p>
<h2>Keep the basket economics explicit</h2>
<p>The retained $622 basket at 34% gross margin has $410.52 fixed goods cost and $211.48 full-price gross profit. A 16% code removes $99.52 of revenue: $522.48 revenue minus $410.52 goods cost leaves $111.96 gross profit. This is an illustration, before shipping, messaging, payment fees, returns and other costs; it is not evidence that a discount causes an incremental order. Compare a no-code reminder with any proposed offer only after both paths restore the same eligible goods.</p>
<h2>Launch from current state, not an old send schedule</h2>
<p>Rebuild a small set of eligible current journeys in the receiving system. An archived scheduled message is an instruction from the past, not a reason to replay a stale promotion. Document the cutover boundary, deduplication key, purchase exit and rollback owner. Verify the new capture surfaces and helpdesk routing, then inspect queue logs for a purchase, opt-out and delayed duplicate event before enabling broader traffic. Set the observation window from event delays and traffic, not an arbitrary migration duration.</p>

</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</section>
</BaseLayout>
